.TH ARM 1 "2026-10-02" "sc-fleet 0.1.0" "sc-fleet manual"
.SH NAME
arm \- re-arm running shells with the current fleet kit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBarm\fR
\fBarm\fR \fB-a\fR | \fB--all\fR
\fBarm\fR \fB-s\fR | \fB--session\fR
\fBarm ls\fR
\fBarm doctor\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A shell keeps the functions and completions it had when it started. Add a
fleet tool and every shell already running is a day behind.
.PP
On 2026-09-21 seven of eight live panes were missing the \fBwt\fR shell
function that had landed the day before; four of those shells were six days
old. The workaround in the shell history was \fBexec zsh\fR, seven times in
one week.
.PP
\fBarm\fR re-sources \fIfleet.zsh\fR, the idempotent subset of the shell
configuration: the fleet's shell functions, the completion \fBfpath\fR entry
and its \fBcompdef\fR registrations, and a \fBrehash\fR so newly installed
binaries become visible.
.SH WHY NOT RE-SOURCE THE RC
Re-sourcing an oh-my-zsh \fI.zshrc\fR is not safe twice. It carries a
powerlevel10k instant-prompt block meant to run once at startup, and its
\fBPATH\fR appends are unguarded, so a second pass duplicates entries and
re-registers prompt hooks. \fIfleet.zsh\fR exists precisely so there is a
subset that \fIis\fR safe to source repeatedly.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS arm
Re-arm this shell. This form is a \fBzsh function\fR, not the binary: a
process cannot change its parent shell, the same wall \fBwt cd\fR hits. The
function sources \fIfleet.zsh\fR in place.
.PP
If the function is missing, the binary prints the path and the one-line
\fBsource\fR command that fixes it, rather than pretending to have acted.
.SS arm -a, arm --all
Re-arm every shell pane in every tmux session.
.SS arm -s, arm --session
Re-arm the panes of the current tmux session only.
.SS arm -a -x, arm -s -x  (also --restart)
Restart each shell instead of re-sourcing it: the sweep sends
\fBexec zsh\fR (or \fBexec bash\fR) to every pane sitting at a prompt.
Re-sourcing refreshes only what is idempotent; a prompt, a plugin style or a
PATH computed at startup needs the shell to start again. State that lives in
the shell (an activated venv, an exported variable, the in-memory history of
that session) is lost, which is why this is a modifier and not the default.
The line is sent under \fBnocorrect\fR so autocorrect cannot touch it.
.SS arm ls
One row per pane: its target, its current command, and whether it would be
armed or skipped and why. Says why rather than silently omitting, because a
pane missing from a list is indistinguishable from a pane that does not
exist.
.SS arm doctor
Whether \fIfleet.zsh\fR is present, correctly linked, parses under
\fBzsh -n\fR, and whether this shell has been through it. Read-only:
diagnoses and names the fix, never repairs.
.SH PANE SELECTION
A pane is armed only when its current command is a shell
(\fBzsh\fR or \fBbash\fR). That field being a shell is the test for "sitting
at a prompt with no foreground job". Editors, pagers and Claude sessions are
skipped, as are panes in copy mode, which swallow keys.
.PP
The pane \fBarm\fR itself runs in is included. Its \fBsource\fR line lands in
the input buffer and runs the moment \fBarm\fR exits, which is why that row
is annotated.
.SH AUTOCORRECT
\fBarm\fR sends \fBsource \fIpath\fR and never a typed command. With zsh
argument autocorrect enabled, in any directory containing \fIsrc\fR the bare
word \fBsc\fR makes zsh stop at
.PP
.RS
correct 'sc' to 'src' [nyae]?
.RE
.PP
which then consumes the following keystrokes as its answer. That corrupted
two command streams on 2026-09-21. Sourcing an absolute path to an existing
file never trips it. If you script against tmux panes yourself, prefix with
\fBnocorrect\fR.
.SH BOOTSTRAP
A shell that started before a tool was installed does not have it in its
command hash, so \fBarm\fR itself may be "command not found" in the very
shell that needs it most. Escape hatches, cheapest first: \fBrehash\fR then
\fBarm\fR; \fBsc arm\fR if \fBsc\fR is already known; \fBarm -a\fR from any
shell that does have it; or \fBexec zsh\fR.
.PP
Once any shell sources \fIfleet.zsh\fR it ends with \fBrehash\fR, so this is
a one-time problem per shell, not a recurring one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ARM_FLEET
Path to the file to source. Default: \fIshell/fleet.zsh\fR in the checkout the tool runs from.
.TP
.B ARM_ART
Force the banner even into a pipe.
.TP
.B ARM_NO_ART \fRor\fB NO_COLOR
Suppress art, or colour, respectively. At the same level "off" beats "on".
.TP
.B ARM_TRACE
Run under \fBset -x\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/arm
The tool, in the sc-fleet checkout.
.TP
.I shell/fleet.zsh
What it sources: the file beside the tool, found through the tool's own
path. \fI.zshrc\fR sources it once at startup as
\fI~/.local/share/sc-fleet/shell/fleet.zsh\fR, the stable link
\fBinstall.sh\fR points at the checkout.
.TP
.I tests/test_arm.sh
The test suite. Run: \fBbash tests/test_arm.sh\fR
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a refusal or when a pane send failed, with a
\fBrefuse\fR or \fBwarn\fR line on stderr saying why. One unreachable pane
does not abort the sweep.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
arm                    # this shell, after a git pull
arm -a                 # every pane, after installing a new fleet tool
arm -a -x              # every pane restarted, after a theme flip or an rc change
arm ls                 # dry look: what would be touched
arm doctor             # is fleet.zsh even linked here
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sc (1),
.BR wt (1),
.BR crew (1),
.BR zshcompsys (1)
